1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,500
It's a quiet Sunday night in France.

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:03,840
The supermarkets are closed.

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:04,940
The lights are off.

4
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:06,600
And inside every store, the fridges are

5
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:09,240
humming, keeping millions of euros of food cold.

6
00:00:09,498 --> 00:00:11,678
Then, one by one, across hundreds of

7
00:00:11,698 --> 00:00:14,078
stores, something strange starts to happen.

8
00:00:14,190 --> 00:00:15,530
The fridges don't break.

9
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:16,550
They don't switch off.

10
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:17,570
They start heating up.

11
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:18,330
Ten degrees.

12
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:19,030
Twenty.

13
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:19,550
Thirty.

14
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:21,610
Until some of them hit fifty degrees Celsius.

15
00:00:21,930 --> 00:00:23,350
That's a hot summer day in the desert,

16
00:00:23,370 --> 00:00:24,410
inside a freezer.

17
00:00:24,701 --> 00:00:26,581
By morning, the food is ruined.

18
00:00:26,801 --> 00:00:29,341
In some stores, there are reports of fires.

19
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:31,573
And nobody broke a window.

20
00:00:31,773 --> 00:00:33,253
Nobody touched a single fridge.

21
00:00:33,273 --> 00:00:35,113
The attack came through a small box on the

22
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:36,913
wall that most people didn't even know was

23
00:00:36,933 --> 00:00:38,313
connected to the internet.

24
00:00:38,505 --> 00:00:39,565
I'm Vio.

25
00:00:39,745 --> 00:00:41,325
And this is the story of the night hackers

26
00:00:41,345 --> 00:00:44,505
turned France's supermarket fridges into ovens.

27
00:00:44,853 --> 00:00:46,453
First, let's talk about how a supermarket

28
00:00:46,473 --> 00:00:48,353
fridge actually works.

29
00:00:48,373 --> 00:00:50,693
Because it's not like the one in your kitchen.

30
00:00:50,795 --> 00:00:52,535
A big supermarket can have dozens of

31
00:00:52,555 --> 00:00:54,715
fridges, freezers and cold rooms.

32
00:00:54,955 --> 00:00:57,055
And all of them are run by one brain.

33
00:00:57,135 --> 00:00:58,495
A refrigeration controller.

34
00:00:58,744 --> 00:01:00,584
It's a little computer, usually sitting in

35
00:01:00,604 --> 00:01:01,564
a back room.

36
00:01:01,744 --> 00:01:03,684
It watches every temperature in the store,

37
00:01:03,704 --> 00:01:05,144
all day, all night.

38
00:01:05,244 --> 00:01:06,564
If a freezer gets a bit too warm,

39
00:01:06,584 --> 00:01:07,484
it fixes it.

40
00:01:07,504 --> 00:01:09,704
If something goes wrong, it sends an alarm.

41
00:01:09,976 --> 00:01:11,234
And here's the important part.

42
00:01:11,254 --> 00:01:14,386
It also runs something called a defrost cycle.

43
00:01:14,431 --> 00:01:16,071
Freezers slowly build up ice on their

44
00:01:16,091 --> 00:01:17,031
cooling coils.

45
00:01:17,271 --> 00:01:18,811
So every few hours, the controller warms

46
00:01:18,831 --> 00:01:20,231
the coils for a short time,

47
00:01:20,251 --> 00:01:22,331
the ice melts, and then the cold comes back.

48
00:01:22,611 --> 00:01:24,311
Normally, that takes maybe an hour.

49
00:01:24,429 --> 00:01:26,929
During that time, the fridge is warm inside.

50
00:01:27,089 --> 00:01:28,469
And that's fine, because the controller

51
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:30,110
knows exactly when to stop.

52
00:01:30,130 --> 00:01:32,050
Unless someone else is giving the orders.

53
00:01:32,238 --> 00:01:34,238
So the controller can make a fridge warm,

54
00:01:34,258 --> 00:01:35,078
on purpose.

55
00:01:35,098 --> 00:01:36,018
Remember that.

56
00:01:36,038 --> 00:01:37,398
Because that's exactly the switch the

57
00:01:37,418 --> 00:01:38,398
hackers pulled.

58
00:01:38,889 --> 00:01:41,069
Now, why would anyone connect this box to

59
00:01:41,089 --> 00:01:41,969
the internet?

60
00:01:42,223 --> 00:01:43,243
Convenience.

61
00:01:43,423 --> 00:01:46,003
A supermarket chain might have hundreds of stores.

62
00:01:46,383 --> 00:01:47,643
The maintenance company can't drive to

63
00:01:47,663 --> 00:01:48,803
every single one.

64
00:01:48,823 --> 00:01:50,263
So the controllers get a web page,

65
00:01:50,283 --> 00:01:51,923
and technicians log in from far away to

66
00:01:51,943 --> 00:01:53,663
check temperatures and change settings.

67
00:01:53,955 --> 00:01:55,135
It saves time.

68
00:01:55,215 --> 00:01:56,315
It saves money.

69
00:01:56,455 --> 00:01:57,655
And when something breaks at three in the

70
00:01:57,675 --> 00:01:59,275
morning, a technician can fix it from

71
00:01:59,295 --> 00:02:00,695
home, in pyjamas.

72
00:02:00,881 --> 00:02:02,821
The problem is, if a technician can reach

73
00:02:02,841 --> 00:02:05,161
that web page from anywhere in the world,

74
00:02:05,221 --> 00:02:06,541
so can anybody else.

75
00:02:06,730 --> 00:02:08,370
Many of these controllers were made by a

76
00:02:08,390 --> 00:02:10,190
Danish company called Danfoss.

77
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:11,690
And the models at the center of this story

78
00:02:11,710 --> 00:02:12,770
are old.

79
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:13,770
Really old.

80
00:02:13,931 --> 00:02:15,771
Danfoss says most of the hacked units were

81
00:02:15,791 --> 00:02:16,871
legacy products.

82
00:02:17,111 --> 00:02:19,371
One model was phased out in 2016.

83
00:02:19,591 --> 00:02:21,991
Another reached its end of life in 2021.

84
00:02:22,291 --> 00:02:24,771
They no longer get software or security updates.

85
00:02:24,998 --> 00:02:26,878
And Danfoss had already published security

86
00:02:26,898 --> 00:02:28,678
warnings about weaknesses in some of its

87
00:02:28,698 --> 00:02:31,338
controllers, in March and August of 2025.

88
00:02:31,738 --> 00:02:33,218
Months before the attack.

89
00:02:33,345 --> 00:02:34,845
But here's the thing about fridges.

90
00:02:35,025 --> 00:02:36,565
When they work, nobody touches them.

91
00:02:36,585 --> 00:02:38,525
These machines can run for many years.

92
00:02:38,605 --> 00:02:40,145
Replacing them costs money.

93
00:02:40,285 --> 00:02:41,765
So old controllers stay on the wall,

94
00:02:41,785 --> 00:02:43,065
long after the company that made them

95
00:02:43,085 --> 00:02:44,305
stopped fixing them.

96
00:02:44,373 --> 00:02:45,633
So picture it.

97
00:02:45,753 --> 00:02:47,413
Thousands of old boxes, running the cold

98
00:02:47,433 --> 00:02:49,393
food of a whole country, with their front

99
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:50,873
door facing the internet,

100
00:02:50,973 --> 00:02:52,673
and no new locks coming.

101
00:02:52,873 --> 00:02:53,433
Ever.

102
00:02:53,992 --> 00:02:56,232
So how did the attackers get in?

103
00:02:56,610 --> 00:02:58,510
Honestly, nobody knows for sure yet.

104
00:02:58,530 --> 00:03:00,570
The investigation is still going on.

105
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:02,700
But security experts keep pointing at the

106
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:03,800
same problem.

107
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:05,740
Old devices, left reachable from the

108
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:08,380
internet, with weak protection and no updates.

109
00:03:08,676 --> 00:03:10,196
And experts had been warning about exposed

110
00:03:10,216 --> 00:03:11,996
equipment like this for years.

111
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:12,696
Not just fridges.

112
00:03:12,716 --> 00:03:14,576
Cameras, factory machines,

113
00:03:14,596 --> 00:03:16,136
even parts of power systems.

114
00:03:16,392 --> 00:03:18,052
One security firm had disclosed weaknesses

115
00:03:18,072 --> 00:03:20,652
in some Danfoss controllers in 2025,

116
00:03:20,672 --> 00:03:21,772
which were later patched.

117
00:03:22,032 --> 00:03:23,632
But nobody has officially confirmed that's

118
00:03:23,652 --> 00:03:24,832
how this attack worked.

119
00:03:25,045 --> 00:03:26,253
And here's what makes it scary.

120
00:03:26,273 --> 00:03:28,655
The attackers didn't need to break the fridges.

121
00:03:28,675 --> 00:03:29,875
They reportedly used a feature that was

122
00:03:29,895 --> 00:03:30,935
already there.

123
00:03:30,996 --> 00:03:32,016
The defrost.

124
00:03:32,076 --> 00:03:33,796
A normal, everyday setting.

125
00:03:33,816 --> 00:03:36,096
Used at the wrong time, for the wrong reason.

126
00:03:36,575 --> 00:03:38,275
And that's what happened on the night of

127
00:03:38,295 --> 00:03:39,447
October eleventh.

128
00:03:39,467 --> 00:03:41,087
The attackers reportedly hijacked a

129
00:03:41,107 --> 00:03:42,607
routine maintenance setting and locked the

130
00:03:42,627 --> 00:03:45,067
fridges into a defrost cycle that didn't end.

131
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:46,707
Instead of one hour, some ran for six

132
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:47,867
hours or more.

133
00:03:48,001 --> 00:03:49,241
And because it happened at night,

134
00:03:49,261 --> 00:03:50,361
while the stores were closed,

135
00:03:50,381 --> 00:03:51,901
there was nobody there to notice.

136
00:03:52,131 --> 00:03:54,611
Hour after hour, the temperature crept up.

137
00:03:54,711 --> 00:03:56,451
One retailer said its cases ran hot for

138
00:03:56,471 --> 00:03:58,831
about six hours before anyone could stop it.

139
00:03:58,865 --> 00:04:00,625
And because thousands of fridges were

140
00:04:00,645 --> 00:04:02,025
attacked in the same night,

141
00:04:02,045 --> 00:04:03,925
this wasn't a broken machine.

142
00:04:04,045 --> 00:04:06,005
It was a coordinated attack.

143
00:04:06,189 --> 00:04:08,649
Temperatures climbed to around fifty degrees.

144
00:04:08,749 --> 00:04:10,449
Meat, fish, milk, cheese,

145
00:04:10,469 --> 00:04:11,369
ice cream.

146
00:04:11,429 --> 00:04:13,189
All of it, slowly cooking inside the cases

147
00:04:13,209 --> 00:04:15,129
that were supposed to protect it.

148
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:16,710
When the first workers walked in on Monday

149
00:04:16,730 --> 00:04:18,310
morning, they found warm freezers,

150
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:20,110
melted food, and in some places,

151
00:04:20,130 --> 00:04:21,230
reports of fires.

152
00:04:21,498 --> 00:04:23,358
Imagine being the store manager.

153
00:04:23,378 --> 00:04:24,958
You open the doors, and instead of cold

154
00:04:24,978 --> 00:04:26,678
air, you get a wall of heat and the smell

155
00:04:26,698 --> 00:04:27,718
of spoiled meat.

156
00:04:27,738 --> 00:04:28,978
Every fridge in the building.

157
00:04:28,998 --> 00:04:30,358
And you have no idea why.

158
00:04:30,459 --> 00:04:32,619
Staff had to throw away tons of food.

159
00:04:32,639 --> 00:04:34,339
And all of it happened before most people

160
00:04:34,359 --> 00:04:35,799
had even had breakfast.

161
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:37,210
And quick favour.

162
00:04:37,230 --> 00:04:38,470
If this kind of story makes you look at

163
00:04:38,490 --> 00:04:39,690
the little boxes around you a bit

164
00:04:39,710 --> 00:04:41,330
differently, hit subscribe.

165
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:43,290
That's how I know you want more of these.

166
00:04:43,926 --> 00:04:46,046
So how big was it?

167
00:04:46,066 --> 00:04:47,426
The numbers keep changing,

168
00:04:47,446 --> 00:04:48,566
but they're all huge.

169
00:04:48,906 --> 00:04:50,286
Intermarché and its sister chain Netto

170
00:04:50,306 --> 00:04:51,646
were among the hardest hit,

171
00:04:51,666 --> 00:04:54,086
with up to three hundred stores reportedly affected.

172
00:04:54,246 --> 00:04:55,506
Carrefour said about eighty of its

173
00:04:55,526 --> 00:04:56,966
supermarkets were attacked.

174
00:04:57,066 --> 00:04:59,166
Monoprix, Leclerc and Coopérative U also

175
00:04:59,186 --> 00:05:00,386
reported problems.

176
00:05:00,714 --> 00:05:02,774
Early counts said a few hundred stores.

177
00:05:03,134 --> 00:05:04,414
Then the French finance minister told

178
00:05:04,434 --> 00:05:06,254
parliament it was about seven hundred,

179
00:05:06,274 --> 00:05:07,474
out of roughly ten thousand supermarkets

180
00:05:07,494 --> 00:05:09,074
in the whole country.

181
00:05:09,164 --> 00:05:11,164
Two supermarkets in Alsace reportedly lost

182
00:05:11,184 --> 00:05:12,624
about two hundred and seventy thousand

183
00:05:12,644 --> 00:05:13,964
euros between them.

184
00:05:14,064 --> 00:05:16,364
Now multiply that by hundreds of stores.

185
00:05:16,609 --> 00:05:18,529
And it wasn't only Danfoss.

186
00:05:18,549 --> 00:05:20,369
France's national cyber agency said

187
00:05:20,389 --> 00:05:21,969
controllers from at least three companies

188
00:05:21,989 --> 00:05:22,889
were hit.

189
00:05:23,009 --> 00:05:24,909
Danfoss, Copeland, and Dixell.

190
00:05:24,938 --> 00:05:27,178
And it may not have stopped at the French border.

191
00:05:27,418 --> 00:05:29,058
There were reports of similar problems in

192
00:05:29,078 --> 00:05:30,358
the Netherlands and Belgium,

193
00:05:30,378 --> 00:05:32,738
although those details are still being checked.

194
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:34,573
France's prime minister asked the agency

195
00:05:34,593 --> 00:05:35,653
to investigate,

196
00:05:35,893 --> 00:05:37,813
and Paris prosecutors opened a case.

197
00:05:38,013 --> 00:05:40,553
And as of now, nobody has claimed responsibility.

198
00:05:40,972 --> 00:05:42,552
Some commentators have pointed to the wave

199
00:05:42,572 --> 00:05:44,272
of so-called hybrid attacks across Europe

200
00:05:44,292 --> 00:05:45,412
in recent years.

201
00:05:45,432 --> 00:05:46,852
But so far, there is no public evidence

202
00:05:46,872 --> 00:05:49,452
linking this attack to any group or country.

203
00:05:49,954 --> 00:05:52,134
Now, if you think fridges are a weird way

204
00:05:52,154 --> 00:05:54,314
to attack someone, this has happened before.

205
00:05:54,489 --> 00:05:57,149
In 2013, hackers stole the card details of

206
00:05:57,169 --> 00:05:58,789
around forty million shoppers from the

207
00:05:58,809 --> 00:06:00,309
American retailer Target.

208
00:06:00,529 --> 00:06:01,489
And the way in?

209
00:06:01,529 --> 00:06:03,149
Login details stolen from a company that

210
00:06:03,169 --> 00:06:05,409
serviced Target's heating and air conditioning.

211
00:06:05,629 --> 00:06:07,549
And sometimes, these attacks don't just

212
00:06:07,569 --> 00:06:08,629
steal data.

213
00:06:08,649 --> 00:06:10,569
They break things in the real world.

214
00:06:10,644 --> 00:06:12,924
Around 2010, a computer worm called

215
00:06:12,944 --> 00:06:14,484
Stuxnet quietly damaged nuclear

216
00:06:14,504 --> 00:06:16,664
centrifuges in Iran, by sending them the

217
00:06:16,684 --> 00:06:17,824
wrong instructions while the screens

218
00:06:17,844 --> 00:06:19,424
showed that everything was fine.

219
00:06:19,548 --> 00:06:21,128
And in 2015,

220
00:06:21,348 --> 00:06:22,768
hackers switched off the power for

221
00:06:22,788 --> 00:06:24,148
hundreds of thousands of people in

222
00:06:24,168 --> 00:06:25,908
Ukraine, in the middle of winter,

223
00:06:25,928 --> 00:06:27,588
by taking over the control systems of

224
00:06:27,608 --> 00:06:28,688
power companies.

225
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:31,120
And there's a famous story from a casino.

226
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:33,100
A cybersecurity company said hackers got

227
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:34,240
into its network through a smart

228
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:36,360
thermometer, in the lobby fish tank.

229
00:06:36,464 --> 00:06:37,444
A fish tank.

230
00:06:37,464 --> 00:06:37,984
A heater.

231
00:06:38,004 --> 00:06:38,664
A fridge.

232
00:06:38,824 --> 00:06:40,564
These are the things nobody thinks about.

233
00:06:40,584 --> 00:06:41,684
Nobody updates them.

234
00:06:41,704 --> 00:06:42,944
And they sit on the same networks as

235
00:06:42,964 --> 00:06:43,964
everything else.

236
00:06:44,122 --> 00:06:45,822
Security people have a saying.

237
00:06:45,842 --> 00:06:47,622
Attackers don't break in through the front door.

238
00:06:47,642 --> 00:06:49,142
They come in through the boiler room.

239
00:06:49,576 --> 00:06:50,936
So what happens now?

240
00:06:51,054 --> 00:06:53,074
France's cyber agency told companies to

241
00:06:53,094 --> 00:06:54,474
take these controllers off the open

242
00:06:54,494 --> 00:06:55,314
internet,

243
00:06:55,454 --> 00:06:57,214
or put them behind a private connection,

244
00:06:57,234 --> 00:06:59,074
and to change any default passwords.

245
00:06:59,329 --> 00:07:01,349
Danfoss says the oldest units simply need

246
00:07:01,369 --> 00:07:02,369
to be replaced.

247
00:07:02,649 --> 00:07:04,269
Which means a lot of supermarkets are

248
00:07:04,289 --> 00:07:06,169
about to buy a lot of new boxes.

249
00:07:06,510 --> 00:07:08,930
And maybe the real lesson is for all of us.

250
00:07:08,950 --> 00:07:10,590
Your router, your smart TV,

251
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:11,730
your doorbell camera.

252
00:07:12,110 --> 00:07:14,050
When did you last update any of them?

253
00:07:14,070 --> 00:07:15,410
Do you even know the password?

254
00:07:15,593 --> 00:07:16,913
So here's my question.

255
00:07:16,973 --> 00:07:18,893
Who should pay for all that ruined food?

256
00:07:19,013 --> 00:07:20,873
The supermarkets, for leaving the door open?

257
00:07:20,893 --> 00:07:22,773
Or the company that made the old boxes?

258
00:07:22,833 --> 00:07:23,873
Tell me in the comments.

259
00:07:24,067 --> 00:07:25,967
And subscribe, because next time,

260
00:07:25,987 --> 00:07:27,267
there's another everyday object that

261
00:07:27,287 --> 00:07:29,047
turned out to be a hacker's way in.

262
00:07:29,113 --> 00:07:30,773
Nobody updated the fridge.

263
00:07:30,793 --> 00:07:33,233
And one night, the fridge became an oven.
